{"reply":"You can acknowledge the effort without agreeing that carrying the burden gives them the only say. Try separating appreciation from participation:

> “I recognize how much work you’ve been carrying, and I appreciate the effort you’ve put into this. I was concerned when the plan changed after our planning meetings ended, because I want to understand the reasoning and have a chance to contribute before decisions are finalized. I’m not questioning your character or commitment—I’m asking for a clearer way for the team to have a say.”

If “I appreciate” feels too strong, you can make it more specific: “I see that you’ve taken on a lot of coordination.” Then keep the focus on the process—how decisions are made and when the team is consulted—rather than on whether they are a good or bad leader.","memory":{"kind":"no_memory_candidate","reason_code":"automatic_capture_disabled"}}

